1. First Identify the Graph Type
A. Undirected Graph

Edges work both ways.

Example:

1 -- 2

Usually phrases like:

“connected”
“friendship”
“roads”
“bidirectional”

Java adjacency list:

graph.get(u).add(v);
graph.get(v).add(u);
B. Directed Graph

Edges have direction.

Example:

1 -> 2

Usually phrases like:

“dependency”
“prerequisite”
“can go from”
“one-way”

Java:

graph.get(u).add(v);
C. Weighted Graph

Edges have costs.

Example:

1 --(5)--> 2

Keywords:

shortest distance
minimum cost
cheapest path

Use:

Dijkstra
Bellman Ford
Floyd Warshall
2. Recognize Problem Keywords
Pattern 1: Traversal Problem

Keywords:

visit all nodes
reachable
connected
path exists

Use:

DFS
BFS
DFS Template
void dfs(int node, boolean[] vis, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> graph) {
    vis[node] = true;

    for(int nbr : graph.get(node)) {
        if(!vis[nbr]) {
            dfs(nbr, vis, graph);
        }
    }
}
BFS Template
Queue<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
q.add(src);
vis[src] = true;

while(!q.isEmpty()) {
    int node = q.remove();

    for(int nbr : graph.get(node)) {
        if(!vis[nbr]) {
            vis[nbr] = true;
            q.add(nbr);
        }
    }
}
Pattern 2: Connected Components

Keywords:

number of groups
provinces
islands
disconnected graph

Idea:
Run DFS/BFS from every unvisited node.

int count = 0;

for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if(!vis[i]) {
        dfs(i, vis, graph);
        count++;
    }
}
Pattern 3: Cycle Detection
Undirected Graph

Keyword:

detect loop
cyclic graph

Use DFS with parent tracking.

boolean dfs(int node, int parent) {
    vis[node] = true;

    for(int nbr : graph.get(node)) {

        if(!vis[nbr]) {
            if(dfs(nbr, node)) return true;
        }

        else if(nbr != parent) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    return false;
}
Directed Graph

Use:

recursion stack
Kahn’s algorithm

Keywords:

dependency cycle
prerequisite impossible

DFS recursion stack:

boolean dfs(int node) {

    vis[node] = true;
    path[node] = true;

    for(int nbr : graph.get(node)) {

        if(!vis[nbr]) {
            if(dfs(nbr)) return true;
        }

        else if(path[nbr]) {
            return true;
        }
    }

    path[node] = false;

    return false;
}
Pattern 4: Topological Sort

Only for DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph)

Keywords:

order of tasks
prerequisites
dependency resolution

Use:

Kahn’s Algorithm (BFS)
DFS topo sort
Kahn’s Algorithm
Queue<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();

for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if(indegree[i] == 0) {
        q.add(i);
    }
}

while(!q.isEmpty()) {
    int node = q.remove();

    for(int nbr : graph.get(node)) {

        indegree[nbr]--;

        if(indegree[nbr] == 0) {
            q.add(nbr);
        }
    }
}
Pattern 5: Shortest Path
Unweighted Graph

Use BFS.

Keywords:

minimum edges
shortest moves
dist[src] = 0;

BFS naturally gives shortest path.

Weighted Graph

Use Dijkstra.

Keywords:

minimum cost
shortest distance
PriorityQueue<Pair> pq =
    new PriorityQueue<>((a,b) -> a.dist - b.dist);
Pattern 6: Grid Problems

Keywords:

matrix
island
rotten oranges
nearest cell

Treat each cell as a graph node.

Movement arrays:

int[] dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
int[] dc = {0, 0, -1, 1};

Usually:

DFS for islands
BFS for shortest time/spread

Examples:

Number of Islands
Rotten Oranges
Flood Fill
Pattern 7: Bipartite Graph

Keywords:

divide into 2 groups
no adjacent same group

Use coloring with BFS/DFS.

color[nbr] = 1 - color[node];

If adjacent nodes have same color → not bipartite.

Pattern 8: Union Find / DSU

Keywords:

dynamic connectivity
redundant connection
merging groups

Useful for:

cycle detection
Kruskal MST

Core idea:

parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
